=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
The fault is a pair of `iptables` FORWARD DROP rules injected into router2's Linux kernel firewall, not a dead RIP daemon as the team hypothesized. Router2's `ripd` process is fully operational: it is sending and receiving RIPv2 updates every 30 seconds, peering correctly with router1 (20.20.0.1), and advertising its directly-connected networks (`1.0.0.0/8`, `128.168.0.0/16`) into the RIP domain. All three routers — router0, router1, and router2 — have complete and correct routing tables for every subnet in the topology.

The actual mechanism: router2's FORWARD chain contains two explicit DROP rules — one matching source `192.168.1.0/24` (IT department) and one matching `192.168.2.0/24` (CS department). When a packet from an IT or CS host transits router2 toward the Server Room (1.0.0.x) or Internet Lab (128.168.0.x), the kernel drops it at the FORWARD hook before it is ever delivered to the destination. Packets originating from Office (192.168.3.x) and Principle (192.168.4.x) are not covered by these rules and are forwarded normally — which is precisely the asymmetry in the symptom. The traceroute from `it_hod` confirms the packet reaches router2 (hop 3 = 20.20.0.2) and then vanishes, and the iptables counters showed 32 packets already dropped for the IT subnet.

Root cause: Two rogue `iptables -A FORWARD -s 192.168.x.0/24 -j DROP` rules were added to router2, selectively blocking forwarded traffic from the IT and CS subnets.

Corrective fix: On router2, run `iptables -D FORWARD -s 192.168.1.0/24 -j DROP` and `iptables -D FORWARD -s 192.168.2.0/24 -j DROP` to remove the offending rules. No RIP or routing changes are needed.
=== END ===